 "status": "speed limits, glitch ceilings and anatomical angle ranges",
 "speed_method": "Per joint, the geodesic angle between consecutive frames of the joint's rest-relative rotation (child segment on parent segment) times the frame rate, on the RAW Rokoko capture (Clip_2..Clip_8, 59,154 frames at 30 Hz, before the retarget's 850 deg/s cap and low-pass). The forearm joint is measured as the motion pipeline and the game carry it on ActorCore bodies (elbow AND pronation: the hand on the humerus with the wrist's flexion and deviation removed); the hand joint as the wrist's flexion and deviation only. The limit is the capture's largest speed over every frame (inside and outside stroke windows; none of the top 20 frames per joint is an out-and-back sensor spike) times the 90th percentile of its 30 Hz under-sampling ratio (a cubic spline of the rotation vector through +-5 frames of each stroke's own peak, resampled at 240 Hz, over 692 strokes; it cannot restore what the suit's own filter removed, so it is a lower bound on the under-read). Left and right share one limit: the larger side's.",
 "glitch_method": "A glitch is a frame where a joint turns faster than its glitch ceiling (the fastest any human source we hold moved it: this player's capture as above, the rigs' 26 authored clips of other actors at the same correction, and the cited mean + 2 SD), or changes its velocity within one frame by more than that ceiling: reaching the ceiling speed from rest in under one 24 fps frame (41.7 ms). The capture never changes a joint's velocity by more than 0.58-0.81 of its own top speed within one frame ('capture_step_change_over_speed_per_frame'), so the second rule never fires on it; it scales as (24 / fps)^2 at another rate. Body synthesis feasibility uses the player's own 'speed_limit_deg_s', not the ceiling.",
 "step_change_method": "A velocity discontinuity: per frame, the angle between consecutive steps (the second difference on SO(3)), at the game's playback (slerp between 30 Hz frames, sampled at 24 fps). The limit is the largest the capture itself shows played back that way, so the capture never exceeds it; it scales as (24 / fps)^2 at another rate.",
